The knowledge service may:

- validate documents and metadata;
- index content;
- retrieve relevant knowledge;
- resolve layered guidance;
- preserve provenance;
- expose freshness and status;
- discover relevant skills;
- explain conflicts;
- compose bounded context packages;
- provide machine-readable references to experiments and decisions.

It must not:

- invent organizational policy;
- silently override source documents;
- collapse unresolved conflicts;
- treat generated summaries as authoritative replacements;
- bypass authorization boundaries.

The service should understand at least:

- principle;
- standard;
- pattern;
- anti-pattern;
- technique;
- skill;
- experiment;
- evaluation;
- decision;
- reference;
- playbook.

Metadata should be validated in CI using a formal schema.

Validation should include:

- unique IDs;
- valid statuses/types;
- valid relationships;
- review metadata;
- unresolved references;
- dependency cycles where prohibited;
- malformed overlay scopes.

Chunking should preserve semantic structure:

- heading hierarchy;
- document ID;
- section path;
- status;
- scope;
- technologies;
- effective dates;
- source version.

Avoid anonymous fragments that cannot be traced back to a precise source section.

Incremental indexing is desirable, but correctness outranks rebuild speed.

The system must invalidate derived material when:

- source content changes;
- metadata changes;
- embedding configuration changes;
- chunking logic changes;
- access scope changes;
- status changes.

The caller should be able to specify or negotiate:

- maximum documents;
- maximum tokens/bytes;
- depth;
- include/exclude evidence;
- include historical material;
- minimum status.

Every candidate guidance item may carry:

- authority;
- trust;
- scope;
- specificity;
- status;
- freshness;
- applicability;
- provenance.

A highly relevant item does not automatically have authority to override policy.

Freshness should support both scheduled and event-driven revalidation.

Events include:

- provider/model changes;
- MCP specification revisions;
- framework/tool releases;
- pricing changes;
- security advisories;
- repeated production failures;
- experiment results;
- scoped policy changes.

MCP exposure introduces an integration boundary.

Current MCP/OpenAI guidance reinforces:

- narrow allowed tool surfaces;
- explicit approval for sensitive operations;
- OAuth/authorization for private data;
- distrust of arbitrary remote MCP servers;
- careful handling of tool outputs as potentially untrusted content.

The handbook knowledge MCP should initially expose read-only capabilities.

Scoped knowledge should use strong tenant boundaries in:

- storage;
- indexes;
- cache keys;
- logs/traces;
- vector namespaces;
- task handles;
- authorization claims.

Measure:

- recall@k;
- precision@k;
- MRR/NDCG where meaningful;
- exact lookup success;
- reranking gain;
- latency;
- index freshness.

Retrieval metrics are proxies.

Also measure:

- agent task success;
- instruction adherence;
- context tokens supplied;
- hallucination/unsupported-claim rate;
- conflict-resolution accuracy;
- human correction rate;
- cost per successful task.

Test:

- cross-scope retrieval attempts;
- scope confusion;
- cache leakage;
- stale/deprecated policy selection;
- malicious content in documents;
- prompt injection embedded in retrieved material.

The service should become the execution environment for many existing context experiments:

- hybrid vs semantic retrieval;
- reranking;
- progressive disclosure;
- context-pack composition;
- freshness labels;
- provenance;
- skill routing.

Revalidate when:

- the MCP spec changes;
- SDK behavior changes;
- retrieval models change;
- embedding/reranker versions change;
- schema changes;
- new scopes/consumers are added;
- security requirements change;
- important handbook guidance is superseded;
- production traces show repeated retrieval/resolution failures.

Evaluate at least:

- task success and correctness;
- reliability across repeated runs;
- tool-use competence;
- instruction following;
- context handling;
- latency, including tail latency;
- input/output/reasoning cost;
- availability and rate limits;
- modality requirements;
- privacy/data-residency requirements;
- provider/tool compatibility;
- operational stability.

- frontier-by-default spending;
- benchmark-driven selection without workload evals;
- changing model and harness simultaneously;
- relying on provider aliases without recording resolved versions;
- evaluating only mean latency and ignoring tail latency;
- routing by cost while omitting failure cost.

For each candidate model/configuration, track observed performance in dimensions that matter to the workload:

- planning;
- coding/edit quality;
- tool selection;
- recovery after failures;
- schema adherence;
- long-context behavior;
- visual reasoning;
- latency;
- cost;
- variance;
- security/authority adherence.

Profiles are empirical and versioned.

Where possible, classify using measurable features rather than subjective labels:

- requested action type;
- number of files/projects implicated;
- presence of failing tests;
- amount of retrieved context;
- tool requirements;
- whether requirements conflict;
- whether the operation has side effects;
- prior attempt failures.

Maintain a small task taxonomy only if it changes an actual decision. Do not create an elaborate ontology before routing/evaluation demonstrates a need.

Capability profiles should be regenerated when:

- a model version changes;
- reasoning defaults change;
- the harness changes materially;
- tool contracts change;
- representative workloads shift.

Small models frequently deserve evaluation for:

- intent/task classification;
- query routing;
- extraction into strict schemas;
- metadata generation;
- ranking and reranking;
- summarization/compaction;
- duplicate detection;
- simple code edits;
- lightweight subagent work;
- relevance screening.

Specialized non-generative models remain especially attractive for embeddings, reranking, classification, and anomaly detection.

Every additional model introduces lifecycle costs:

- provider integration;
- eval coverage;
- versioning;
- fallback behavior;
- observability;
- security review;
- capacity/rate limits;
- routing complexity.

A specialized model should earn its place through measurable improvement.

Likely candidates:

- ambiguous debugging;
- architecture decisions;
- complex multi-step planning;
- security/code review;
- tasks with conflicting evidence;
- recovery after repeated failed attempts.

Likely poor candidates:

- deterministic extraction;
- simple classification;
- formatting;
- straightforward transformation;
- operations already validated by code.

Potential signals include:

- deterministic task type;
- requested modality;
- context volume;
- tool requirements;
- risk class;
- latency SLO;
- budget;
- prior failures;
- small-model uncertainty;
- learned predicted quality;
- expected incremental gain from escalation.

Trace:

- router inputs/features;
- chosen model/configuration;
- alternatives considered where available;
- escalation reason;
- outcome;
- cost and latency.

Otherwise routing regressions are difficult to diagnose.

Useful escalation signals include:

- compilation/test failure;
- invalid schema;
- unresolved tool errors;
- missing required evidence;
- objective grader failure;
- repeated non-progress;
- calibrated router score.

Model-generated confidence alone is generally too weak to be the sole escalation signal unless calibrated for the workload.

Escalation should transfer:

- goal;
- constraints;
- verified discoveries;
- failed attempts;
- test/tool evidence;
- current workspace state.

Do not transfer every speculative conclusion as fact.

- availability fallback;
- rate-limit/capacity fallback;
- regional/compliance fallback;
- capability fallback;
- cost/budget fallback.

Fallbacks should not silently change the semantic contract of the workflow.

Potential differences include:

- tool-call semantics;
- reasoning controls;
- context behavior;
- structured-output behavior;
- caching;
- safety behavior;
- rate limits;
- observability;
- data-retention controls.

Adapters should surface meaningful differences rather than hide them.

Evaluate:

- quality on representative tasks;
- hardware/capacity requirements;
- concurrency;
- latency distribution;
- model loading/cold start;
- quantization impact;
- operational staffing;
- patching/security;
- data residency;
- licensing;
- total cost of ownership.

Self-hosted models may be attractive for:

- embeddings;
- reranking;
- classification;
- sensitive local retrieval;
- high-volume bounded transformations;
- offline development environments.

General-purpose autonomous coding is a higher bar and should be benchmarked directly.

Track a vector, not a single score:

- task success;
- correctness/tests;
- human intervention;
- unsafe/unauthorized actions;
- retries;
- tool calls;
- input/output/reasoning tokens;
- cost;
- time to first useful output;
- end-to-end latency;
- tail latency;
- variance.

Record:

- exact model/version or resolved alias;
- reasoning effort;
- harness commit;
- tool versions;
- environment resources;
- dataset version;
- evaluator version.

Re-run relevant evals when:

- a model version changes;
- a provider changes default reasoning effort;
- pricing changes materially;
- context/tool capabilities change;
- the harness changes;
- tool schemas change;
- safety/permission behavior changes;
- production traces show regression;
- a new candidate model becomes economically interesting.

Each model-specific recommendation should link to:

- supporting evals;
- date/model version;
- affected workloads;
- owner;
- review trigger;
- replacement/deprecation information.

Historical results stay searchable because they explain why architecture changed.

No single defense is sufficient. Prefer overlapping controls:
- trustworthy instruction hierarchy;
- explicit trust labels for external content;
- least-privilege tools and credentials;
- filesystem/network isolation;
- structured validation at tool boundaries;
- human or policy approval for consequential actions;
- deterministic authorization checks;
- traceability and security evals;
- rollback and recovery mechanisms.

Common zones include:
- user or calling service;
- trusted application policy;
- model runtime;
- retrieved documents and web content;
- tools/MCP servers;
- sandbox;
- internal systems;
- third-party services;
- persistent memory;
- production systems.

Whenever information or authority crosses a boundary, define:
- authentication;
- authorization;
- validation;
- provenance;
- confidentiality expectations;
- logging;
- failure behavior.

- separate trusted instructions from untrusted data;
- reduce untrusted text to structured fields when possible;
- propagate provenance/trust labels;
- restrict the tools available during untrusted-content processing;
- isolate sensitive credentials and network destinations;
- require approval for consequential side effects;
- verify that proposed actions align with user intent independently of the untrusted source.

Where feasible, distinguish:
- the human principal;
- the agent/workload identity;
- the tool/service identity;
- the target-resource authorization context.

This enables accountability and prevents one shared credential from becoming universal authority.

- never embed secrets in prompts, memory, or reusable handbook content;
- inject credentials at the execution boundary;
- redact secrets from traces and tool output;
- rotate and revoke credentials independently of agent configuration;
- prefer delegated/OAuth/workload identity mechanisms to copied long-lived tokens.

- production deployment or destructive mutation;
- sending external communications;
- deleting or overwriting important data;
- modifying access controls;
- creating financial commitments;
- disclosure of sensitive information;
- security-sensitive actions outside a preapproved engagement scope.

High-impact actions should verify:
- target/environment;
- principal and authorization;
- current state;
- expected impact;
- backup/rollback or compensating action;
- concurrency and idempotency constraints;
- required approval.

Watch for memories or knowledge entries that:
- lack provenance;
- unexpectedly expand scope;
- instruct future agents rather than describe facts;
- originate in low-trust external sources;
- survive beyond their intended task/scope lifetime.

Record where appropriate:
- principal/workload identity;
- trust/source classification of important inputs;
- tool requested and arguments;
- authorization decision;
- approval decision;
- credential/capability used (identifier, not secret);
- side-effect result;
- denied attempts;
- network destination/resource target;
- memory writes;
- policy/version identifiers.

Every meaningful incident or near miss should be evaluated for promotion into:
- a regression case;
- a new threat pattern;
- a policy change;
- a tool-contract change;
- a handbook update.

Initial default review intervals:
- durable least-privilege principles: ~180d;
- tool/auth architecture: ~90d;
- provider/MCP security assumptions: ~60d;
- model-specific prompt-injection mitigations: ~30–60d;
- active incident controls: event-driven.

These are defaults, not guarantees.

Loading a skill does not imply loading every supporting file.

The agent should retrieve deeper material only when required.

Examples:

- load a schema when producing structured output;
- load a migration reference when performing a migration;
- run a helper script instead of reading its implementation unless inspection is required.

- ambiguous descriptions cause incorrect activation;
- overlapping skills create routing conflicts;
- giant SKILL.md files recreate static-context bloat;
- nested references become difficult to discover;
- capability catalogs themselves consume excessive context;
- a skill remains loaded after it is no longer relevant.

- purpose and activation conditions;
- required inputs;
- workflow or decision procedure;
- constraints and non-goals;
- required tool behavior;
- verification requirements;
- stop/escalation conditions;
- pointers to deeper resources.

It should not contain every possible example, reference manual, or enterprise standard.

If a deterministic operation can be represented reliably in code, prefer a tested script over natural-language instructions telling the model to reproduce the operation manually.

Examples:

- normalize JSON;
- validate frontmatter;
- generate a dependency report;
- check repository policy;
- transform known formats.

Distinguish:

- required capability;
- optional enhancement;
- reference-only relationship;
- ordering dependency;
- tool dependency;
- policy dependency.

If multiple skills provide conflicting guidance:

1. higher-authority external policy wins;
2. repository/scope constraints are applied;
3. the conflict should be surfaced rather than silently merged;
4. skill-specific advice may not override canonical standards without an explicit allowed override.

Keep provider-neutral:

- purpose;
- activation conditions;
- workflow logic;
- standards references;
- input/output contracts;
- verification requirements;
- examples;
- deterministic scripts where portable;
- evals.

Provider-specific behavior may include:

- file location;
- manifest metadata;
- invocation syntax;
- discovery registration;
- sandbox layout;
- provider-specific tool names;
- provider-specific workarounds.

Provider adapters require shorter review intervals than canonical skill logic.

Any workaround for a provider/model limitation should include:

- reason;
- originating failure/eval;
- compatibility range if known;
- removal criteria;
- review date.

Did the correct skill activate?

Metrics may include:

- activation precision;
- activation recall;
- unnecessary activation rate;
- conflicting activation rate.

Did the skill improve the task?

Measure:

- task success;
- adherence to required process;
- tool choice;
- verification completeness;
- retries;
- latency;
- token/cost impact.

Test:

- incomplete inputs;
- conflicting repository rules;
- unavailable tools;
- adversarial content;
- unsupported environments;
- provider/model changes.

For managed the platform skills, record:

- owner;
- source;
- version;
- status;
- checksum/signature where infrastructure supports it;
- review history;
- required permissions;
- scripts/executable content;
- external dependencies;
- linked standards;
- eval status.

Scripts should receive normal software security treatment:

- source review;
- dependency review;
- least privilege;
- sandboxing;
- deterministic invocation;
- explicit inputs/outputs.

A skill description does not grant its scripts additional authority.

A handbook topic should not automatically become a skill.

Create a skill when:

- the workflow recurs;
- procedural consistency matters;
- instructions are sufficiently stable;
- activation conditions can be described;
- required inputs and outputs are known;
- success can be evaluated;
- packaging reduces repeated explanation or errors.

- knowledge is primarily explanatory;
- procedure is too unstable;
- task remains highly bespoke;
- live authoritative state is the real requirement;
- deterministic software would be better;
- the skill would merely duplicate repository/scope instructions.

Version changes should communicate behavioral compatibility.

Major changes may include:

- changed goal or activation semantics;
- new authority requirements;
- incompatible input/output contract;
- materially different workflow.

Minor changes may include backward-compatible procedure improvements.

Patch changes may include wording, examples, or bug fixes that do not intentionally change the contract.

Revalidate when:

- a referenced standard changes;
- a tool/MCP contract changes;
- a model generation changes materially;
- a provider changes skill-loading behavior;
- a language/framework ecosystem changes;
- a recurring production failure appears;
- security guidance changes;
- a skill is repeatedly not selected or selected incorrectly.

Deprecation should preserve:

- why the skill existed;
- what superseded it;
- migration guidance;
- historical eval evidence.

the platform should prefer tools when information or actions are better obtained from the environment than described in prompts.

Use a tool when it provides one or more of:

- current state;
- deterministic computation;
- authoritative data;
- external actions;
- reproducible verification;
- access to data too large or volatile for permanent context;
- encapsulation of credentials or privileged operations.

Do not create a tool merely to hide a trivial deterministic transformation that belongs in ordinary application code.

Read and write behavior should normally be distinguishable at the tool-contract level.

This supports:

- clearer model selection;
- better approvals;
- safer retries;
- more precise authorization;
- easier auditing;
- more meaningful traces.

A tool that sometimes reads and sometimes mutates based on loosely interpreted input increases ambiguity.

A strong model cannot compensate reliably for poorly named, overlapping, ambiguous, overpowered, or undocumented tools.

Common symptoms of bad tool design include:

- repeated wrong-tool selection;
- hallucinated parameters;
- excessive exploratory calls;
- inability to recover from errors;
- fragile retries;
- excessive tool output;
- security approvals that are too broad to be meaningful.

Tool design should therefore be evaluated as part of agent design, not delegated entirely to API owners.

Tool guidance is version-sensitive. Each tool standard should record:

- contract version;
- provider/protocol version where relevant;
- last validation date;
- deprecated fields/behaviors;
- known client compatibility;
- associated evals;
- migration notes.

A previously successful tool contract may become suboptimal as models improve. Tool descriptions, schemas, granularity, and discovery mechanisms should be periodically re-evaluated rather than indefinitely accumulating compatibility text.

Descriptions are not documentation only. They influence tool selection.

A good description should answer:

- what goal this tool serves;
- when to use it;
- when not to use it;
- important side effects;
- prerequisites the caller must already have.

Avoid descriptions that overlap heavily with sibling tools.

Structured output is especially valuable when results will be:

- filtered;
- joined;
- ranked;
- cached;
- validated;
- passed to another tool;
- consumed by deterministic orchestration.

Example:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "commit": "abc123",
  "changed_files": ["src/a.ts"],
  "warnings": []
}
```

Prefer compact structured results plus references to large artifacts rather than dumping large logs into model context.

Every production tool should have tests for:

- valid inputs;
- invalid schemas;
- semantic invalidity;
- permission denial;
- missing resources;
- transient failures;
- unexpected provider responses;
- output schema stability;
- side-effect behavior;
- idempotent retries where promised.

Keep tools permanently visible when they are:

- frequently required;
- foundational for discovery;
- cheap in schema/context size;
- low ambiguity;
- necessary for safety or user interaction.

Examples might include repository search, reading known files, or requesting user approval in a coding harness.

Compact metadata should include:

- name;
- short purpose;
- capability domain;
- read/write classification;
- required scope;
- risk class;
- version;
- optional keywords/examples.

Do not place full operating manuals in discovery metadata.

The capability catalog may be narrowed based on:

- current repository;
- client;
- user role;
- task type;
- environment;
- connected systems;
- authorization grants.

Dynamic scoping is both a context optimization and a security-supporting measure, but hidden capabilities are not an authorization boundary.

Watch for:

- a needed tool becoming undiscoverable;
- excessive discovery calls;
- stale capability metadata;
- tool aliases causing ambiguity;
- discovery exposing capabilities the caller cannot actually use;
- dynamic scoping hiding a legitimate recovery path.

Write operations should be idempotent where practical.

Possible strategies:

- idempotency keys;
- optimistic concurrency/version checks;
- deduplication tokens;
- compare-and-set operations;
- explicit current-state checks.

Current OpenAI programmatic tool guidance specifically recommends idempotent function calls because replay/retry should not duplicate unsafe side effects.

Retries must be bounded by:

- attempt count;
- elapsed time;
- monetary cost;
- side-effect risk;
- user-visible latency.

Repeated model reasoning around the same provider error should not become an unbounded loop.

Authorization should be scoped to the smallest practical combination of:

- identity;
- tenant/scope;
- resource;
- action;
- environment;
- time;
- purpose where enforceable.

Do not pass broad credentials to the model when a service can hold them and expose a narrower capability.

Prefer distinct read/write tools when doing so improves:

- permission scoping;
- approval clarity;
- auditing;
- retry behavior;
- model selection.

High-impact calls should record:

- caller identity;
- agent/session identity;
- tool/version;
- normalized parameters;
- authorization decision;
- approval evidence;
- result/status;
- correlation/operation ID.

Do not log secrets merely for observability.

Use MCP primitives according to their semantics rather than modeling everything as a tool.

Conceptually:

- **tools** expose actions/capabilities;
- **resources** expose retrievable contextual material;
- **prompts** expose reusable prompt templates/workflows where supported and appropriate.

The handbook itself may eventually expose guidance primarily as resources/search capabilities while executable operations remain tools.

For enterprise deployments, an MCP gateway may provide:

- authentication;
- authorization;
- routing;
- rate limiting;
- tenant isolation;
- telemetry;
- policy enforcement;
- tool allowlists;
- version compatibility.

A gateway should not become a place where every domain concern is reimplemented.

MCP is preferred when multiple permission scopes benefit from the same capability, but direct native tools may remain appropriate when:

- only one runtime needs the capability;
- latency is critical;
- MCP adds no interoperability value;
- the provider offers stronger native semantics needed by the workload.

Cancellation must define whether it is:

- guaranteed;
- best-effort;
- unsupported after a phase boundary;
- compensating rather than truly cancelling.

Do not tell a model that a task was cancelled merely because a cancel request was accepted.

Useful metrics include:

- selection precision/recall where a gold set exists;
- unnecessary tool calls;
- call count per successful task;
- argument validation failures;
- permission denials;
- transient/permanent error rates;
- retry success;
- p50/p95 latency;
- output token/context volume;
- approval frequency;
- downstream task success;
- cost.

A strong eval suite should include cases where the correct behavior is **not** to call a tool.

Examples:

- user asks an explanatory question requiring no external state;
- requested write exceeds authority;
- untrusted content instructs the agent to call an unrelated tool;
- destructive action lacks approval;
- duplicate request should be recognized as already completed.

A tool description or granularity optimized for one generation of models may be unnecessary or harmful with newer models.

Re-run representative tool-selection and recovery evals when:

- default models change materially;
- schema or descriptions change;
- tool catalog size changes;
- authorization policy changes;
- provider/tool-calling semantics change.

Revalidation should be event-driven as well as scheduled.

Trigger review when:

- the underlying API changes;
- a provider changes tool-calling behavior;
- MCP publishes a new stable specification;
- security advisories affect the capability;
- production traces reveal repeated misuse;
- task success regresses;
- new models make workaround instructions obsolete;
- latency/cost changes materially.

Where possible, CI should verify:

- schemas still validate;
- examples compile/run;
- referenced endpoints/spec versions still exist;
- permission assumptions still hold;
- capability discovery snapshots are expected;
- contract fixtures still pass.

Future tooling may also flag handbook documents whose `last_reviewed` exceeds `review_interval`.

Deprecation should include:

- reason;
- replacement;
- compatibility window;
- migration instructions;
- removal date if applicable.

The MCP project itself now has a formal deprecation policy with a minimum compatibility window, reinforcing the need to design integrations for change rather than assuming protocol permanence.

Instructions added because a particular model misunderstood a tool should not silently become permanent architecture.

Tag workaround guidance with:

- model/provider scope;
- date introduced;
- associated failure/eval;
- review trigger.

Delete it when it stops earning its complexity.

Every production-grade tool capability should be traceable to:

- an owner;
- a contract version;
- a documented authority model;
- executable contract tests;
- representative agent-tool evals;
- a review date or review trigger;
- deprecation/migration semantics.

